FAQ = [
 ("What is the best trailer hitch for a 2021+ Ford Bronco?",
  "For most owners, the Draw-Tite 76527. It is a Class III with a 2 in receiver rated 4,500 lb gross trailer weight and 675 lb tongue weight, which covers the Bronco Raptor's 4,500 lb rating as well as the 3,500 lb rating on most other builds. Draw-Tite quotes a 20-minute bolt-on install with no drilling, and its extended receiver helps with the tailgate spare. The CURT 13493 is the lower-cost alternative rated 3,500 lb."),
 ("How much can a Ford Bronco tow?",
  "Most 2021–2026 Broncos are rated at up to 3,500 lb when properly equipped. The Bronco Raptor with the 3.0L EcoBoost is rated at 4,500 lb. Some trims and packages are lower: an etrailer expert answer cites 3,080 lb for a 2024 Everglades, and dealer charts list ranges from about 3,080 to 3,500 lb by year and configuration. Check the towing chart in your owner's manual for your exact build."),
 ("Does every Bronco come with a factory hitch?",
  "No. Owners on Bronco6G report that the receiver comes only when the Trailer Tow Package was ticked on the order, and that trucks without it, including a Badlands Sasquatch in that thread, have no hitch. Trailer tow prep wiring is standard, per the same thread. Look under the rear bumper: if a square receiver is already there, you only need a ball mount and possibly the harness."),
 ("Will a Bronco Sport hitch fit a Bronco?",
  "No. The Bronco Sport is a separate compact crossover on a different platform, and its hitches are different parts: Draw-Tite sells the 76436 for the Bronco Sport and the 76527 and 76605 for the full-size Bronco. Every listing on this page names the full-size Bronco, and several say \"not for Bronco Sport\" in the title. Check that wording before you order."),
 ("Is the Sasquatch package the same as the tow package?",
  "No. The Sasquatch package is about off-road hardware: a mild lift and 35 in tires. Whether a Sasquatch truck has a receiver depends on whether the Trailer Tow Package was ordered too. One Bronco6G owner with a Badlands Sasquatch reported no factory hitch. The bigger tires don't change the aftermarket hitch fit on this page, but they do make the spare larger, so check rack clearance."),
 ("Do I need to remove the rear bumper to install a Bronco hitch?",
  "Usually you loosen or remove bumper bolts, not the whole bumper. The TrailBronco install of a Rough Country hitch removed two 13 mm body-mount bolts and four 18 mm bolts under the bumper and unplugged a harness on the driver's side. etrailer notes you may need to loosen the rear bumper to reach bracket bolts. Draw-Tite quotes 20 minutes and the Ford receiver kit about an hour."),
 ("What's the difference between Draw-Tite 76527 and 76605?",
  "Both are Class III, 2 in hitches rated 4,500 lb and 675 lb for the 2021–2026 Bronco. Draw-Tite lists the 76527 as the extended receiver length and the 76605 as the standard length. The extended version puts the receiver opening further back, which gives a hitch rack more room before it meets the tailgate spare. The standard one tucks closer to the bumper."),
 ("What wiring do I need to tow with a Bronco?",
  "A 4-way flat connector covers lights on most small trailers. A braked trailer needs a 7-way connector and an electric brake controller. Ford's receiver kit says a wiring harness is sold separately, and Ford's accessory store lists a 4/7-pin harness for the Bronco. Bronco6G owners note that adding the hitch later doesn't give you the factory trailer brake controller, so budget for an aftermarket one if you tow a braked trailer."),
 ("Can I use a hitch rack with the Bronco's tailgate spare?",
  "Yes, but the spare sets the clearance. The Bronco's spare hangs on the swing-out tailgate above the receiver, and the tailgate swings sideways, so a loaded rack can block it. Choose an extended receiver like the Draw-Tite 76527, or a swing-away rack, and measure from the pin hole to the back of the tire. CURT notes its 13493 is not compatible with vertical-hanging bike racks."),
 ("Is a 3,500 lb hitch enough for a Bronco?",
  "For every Bronco except the Raptor, yes. Most builds are rated at or below 3,500 lb, so a 3,500 lb hitch like the CURT 13493 or the Rough Country matches the vehicle. The Raptor is rated at 4,500 lb, and a 3,500 lb hitch would become the limit. Raptor owners should choose a 4,500 lb hitch such as the Draw-Tite 76527 or 76605."),
]

